(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["inference",{"l:inference/what-happens-when-you-call-a-model":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A model call is a pipeline. The server turns your messages into tokens with the chat template; \u003cb>prefill\u003c/b> runs the whole prompt through the model in one parallel pass, filling the \u003cb>KV cache\u003c/b> and producing the first output token; \u003cb>decode\u003c/b> then generates one token per forward pass, each pass reading the whole cache; tokens are turned back into text and streamed until a \u003cb>stop condition\u003c/b>. Every cost and latency number you see maps to one of these steps.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The path of a request\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Messages and tools\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Template and tokenise\u003c/b>\u003csmall>text to token IDs\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Prefix cache lookup\u003c/b>\u003csmall>reuse stored KV?\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Prefill\u003c/b>\u003csmall>whole prompt in one pass\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>First token\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Decode step\u003c/b>\u003csmall>one token in, read KV cache\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Sample next token\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Detokenise and stream\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Stop?\u003c/b>\u003csmall>end token, stop string, max_tokens\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Step by step:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Admission.\u003c/b> The gateway checks your key and rate limits and routes the request to a replica serving the model. Under load it may wait in a queue; that wait is part of your latency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Template and tokenise.\u003c/b> System prompt, tool definitions and messages are rendered with the model's chat template and tokenised. This takes milliseconds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prefix cache lookup.\u003c/b> If a recent request started with exactly the same tokens, the server can reuse their stored keys and values and skip that part of prefill. This is what provider prompt caching sells (lesson 9).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prefill.\u003c/b> The remaining prompt tokens go through every layer in one batched forward pass. Two outputs: the keys and values of every prompt token in every layer (the KV cache), and the logits at the last position.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>First token.\u003c/b> The sampler turns the last position's logits into a token. The time until this point is the \u003cb>time to first token (TTFT)\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Decode loop.\u003c/b> The new token is fed back in alone. Each layer computes its query, key and value, appends the key and value to the cache and attends over all cached entries. The logits give the next token. One forward pass per output token, strictly in sequence.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Detokenise and stream.\u003c/b> Token IDs become text as they arrive. A token can be part of a multi-byte character (an emoji or a Hindi letter may span several byte-level tokens), so servers hold bytes back until a full character is ready, and they hold back text that might be the start of a stop string.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stop.\u003c/b> Generation ends when the model emits its end-of-turn token, the output matches a stop sequence, the output reaches \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>, or the model emits a complete tool call.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Accounting.\u003c/b> The response reports usage: prompt tokens (and how many were cached), output tokens, and for reasoning models the hidden reasoning tokens counted inside output.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Stop reasons\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Always read why generation stopped. Names differ by API; the meanings do not:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\u003cth>OpenAI Chat Completions \u003ccode>finish_reason\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Anthropic Messages \u003ccode>stop_reason\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Model finished its turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stop\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>end_turn\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hit the output token limit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>length\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Matched one of your stop sequences\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stop\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stop_sequence\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Wants to call a tool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tool_calls\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tool_use\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A \u003ccode>length\u003c/code> stop means the output is truncated: half a JSON object, a sentence cut mid-word, or a reasoning model that used its whole budget thinking and never answered.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where the time goes\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For a typical chat request the phases have very different costs. As an illustration, an 8B model on one modern data-centre GPU might prefill a 2,000-token prompt in under 0.1 seconds and then spend about 5 milliseconds on each output token, so a 300-token answer takes about 1.5 seconds of decode. Output length, not prompt length, usually dominates total time. The next lesson explains why the two phases differ so much.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stream\u003c/b> responses to users: decode is sequential, so streaming makes the first words appear after TTFT instead of after the whole answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Check the stop reason in code\u003c/b> and treat \u003ccode>length\u003c/code> as an error or a retry with a larger limit, especially for JSON.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reasoning tokens are decode tokens.\u003c/b> A reasoning model that thinks for 2,000 tokens pays the decode time and the output price for every one of them before you see a word.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Count what you send.\u003c/b> System prompts and tool schemas go through prefill on every call unless a prefix cache hits.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does prefill produce?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The keys and values of every prompt token in every layer (the KV cache), and the logits at the last prompt position, from which the first output token is sampled.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why might a streamed response pause briefly or arrive in uneven chunks even when the model generates at a steady rate?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The server holds back text while a token is only part of a multi-byte character or while the recent output could be the start of a stop sequence; network buffering and batching of server-sent events add unevenness too.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your JSON output is cut off mid-object. What does the stop reason most likely say, and what do you do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>length\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>): the output hit the token limit. Raise the limit, shorten what you ask for, or detect the stop reason and retry; for reasoning models also check whether hidden reasoning consumed the budget.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name four conditions that end generation.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model emits its end-of-turn token; the output matches a stop sequence; the output reaches the maximum token limit; the model emits a complete tool call (and in some APIs, a safety or content filter stop).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:inference/prefill-versus-decode":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Prefill processes every prompt token at once, so each weight read from memory is used for thousands of tokens and the GPU's arithmetic units are the bottleneck (\u003cb>compute-bound\u003c/b>). Decode produces one token per sequence per step, so the whole model must be read from memory to do a tiny amount of arithmetic, and memory bandwidth is the bottleneck (\u003cb>memory-bound\u003c/b>). That is why output tokens are slower and usually cost several times more than input tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Arithmetic intensity in one paragraph\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A GPU can do a certain number of operations per second and move a certain number of bytes per second from its memory. An NVIDIA H100 does roughly 990 trillion 16-bit floating-point operations (FLOPs) per second on dense matrix work and reads about 3.35 TB per second from its high-bandwidth memory: about \u003cb>300 FLOPs per byte\u003c/b>. Work that does fewer FLOPs per byte of data moved waits on memory; work that does more waits on arithmetic. That ratio for your workload is its \u003cb>arithmetic intensity\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Multiplying a token's vector by a weight matrix costs 2 FLOPs per weight (a multiply and an add), and each 16-bit weight is 2 bytes. So:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>1 token  through the weights:  2 FLOPs per 2 bytes   -&gt;   about 1 FLOP per byte   (memory-bound)\nN tokens through the weights:  weights read once, used N times  -&gt;  about N FLOPs per byte\n\nPrefill of 2,000 tokens:       ~2,000 FLOPs per byte  -&gt;  compute-bound\nDecode, 1 sequence:            ~1 FLOP per byte       -&gt;  memory-bound, GPU arithmetic ~99% idle\nDecode, batch of 64:           ~64 FLOPs per byte     -&gt;  still memory-bound\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>To make decode compute-bound on an H100 you need a few hundred sequences decoding together (the scaling book puts the threshold at about 280 for 16-bit weights), which is why servers batch so aggressively (lesson 8).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example: Llama 3.1 8B on one H100\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Weights: 8.03 billion parameters × 2 bytes = 16.1 GB.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Decode, one user.\u003c/b> Each step reads all 16.1 GB of weights: 16.1 GB ÷ 3.35 TB/s = 4.8 ms, so at most about 210 tokens per second. Real systems reach perhaps 70 to 80% of peak bandwidth. Each step must also read this conversation's KV cache, which grows with context (lessons 3 and 4):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Context so far\u003c/th>\u003cth>KV cache read per step\u003c/th>\u003cth>Time per token (bandwidth limit)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Upper bound\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>0 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.8 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>209 tokens/s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>8,192 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.1 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5.1 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>196 tokens/s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>32,768 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.3 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6.1 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>165 tokens/s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>131,072 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>17.2 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>9.9 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>101 tokens/s\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Prefill, 2,000 tokens.\u003c/b> About 2 × 8.03 billion × 2,000 = 32 trillion FLOPs. At a realistic 50% of 990 TFLOPS that is about 65 ms, or 0.03 ms per token.\u003c/p>\u003cp>So one input token costs about 0.03 ms of GPU time and one output token about 4.8 ms: a factor of around 150 for a single user. Batching narrows the gap for output tokens, but decode always uses the hardware far less efficiently than prefill.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What this explains\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>What you observe\u003c/th>\u003cth>Explanation\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Output tokens priced above input tokens (as of late 2026, often around 4 to 5 times)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each output token needs its own sequential, memory-bound pass\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Long prompts raise time to first token\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefill work grows with prompt length, and attention's share grows with its square\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tokens per second fall in long conversations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Every decode step reads the whole, growing KV cache\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cached input tokens are cheap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Their prefill is skipped; stored keys and values are reused\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Batch APIs are about half price\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The provider can pack requests into large, efficient batches when it suits them\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Speculative decoding speeds up generation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Spare compute in memory-bound decode is used to check several guessed tokens per weight read (Serving module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Large providers separate prefill and decode GPUs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The two phases have opposite bottlenecks and interfere when mixed (disaggregated serving, Serving module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>On your own machine, memory bandwidth predicts speed.\u003c/b> Decode tokens per second is roughly memory bandwidth divided by model size in bytes. A 4-bit 8B model (about 5 GB) on a laptop with 120 GB/s of bandwidth tops out near 24 tokens per second; a faster chip with four times the bandwidth gives about four times the speed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Long prompts compete with ongoing decodes.\u003c/b> A server that prefills a 100,000-token prompt in one go stalls every user's decode for that long. Servers use \u003cb>chunked prefill\u003c/b>, splitting long prompts into pieces interleaved with decode steps (the Sarathi-Serve idea).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reasoning effort is a decode cost.\u003c/b> Thinking tokens are generated one at a time, so high effort settings cost both money and wall-clock time.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is decode memory-bound at batch size 1?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Producing one token requires reading every weight once but uses each weight for only one multiply-add, about 1 FLOP per byte read. GPUs can do around 300 FLOPs in the time it takes to read one byte, so the arithmetic units sit idle waiting for memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Estimate the maximum decode speed of a 4-bit 8B model (about 5 GB) on a laptop with 120 GB/s of memory bandwidth.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 120 ÷ 5 = 24 tokens per second as an upper bound; in practice somewhat less, and lower still at long contexts because the KV cache must be read too.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can processing 2,000 prompt tokens take less time than generating 50 output tokens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prompt tokens are processed together, so the weights are read once and used 2,000 times (compute-bound, about 65 ms for an 8B model on an H100). Each output token needs its own full read of the weights (about 5 ms each), so 50 tokens take about 240 ms.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does batching raise throughput without slowing each user much, up to a point?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In memory-bound decode, the weights read for one sequence can serve many sequences in the same step for little extra time. Each step still costs roughly one weight read plus each sequence's KV cache, so tokens per second across all users rises almost linearly with batch size until the KV reads, memory capacity or compute become the limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:context-windows-and-the-kv-cache":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>To generate the next token, attention needs the keys and values of every earlier token in every layer. Those never change once computed, because the causal mask stops later tokens influencing earlier ones, so the server stores them: the \u003cb>KV cache\u003c/b>. Each new token then costs one forward pass for one token instead of re-running the whole sequence. The price is memory: the cache grows by a fixed amount with every token of context, for every request in flight.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What exactly is cached\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every layer, every key-value head and every token so far, the cache holds one key vector and one value vector of \u003ccode>head_dim\u003c/code> numbers. Queries are not cached: only the newest token's query is ever needed. Nothing else is kept between steps (no MLP activations, no old logits).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>               token 1   token 2   token 3   ...   token n\nlayer 1    K [  k        k         k               k  ]     V [  v   v   v  ...  v  ]\nlayer 2    K [  k        k         k               k  ]     V [  v   v   v  ...  v  ]\n ...\nlayer 32   K [  k        k         k               k  ]     V [  v   v   v  ...  v  ]\n\neach k or v entry = kv_heads × head_dim numbers  (8 × 128 = 1,024 numbers in Llama 3.1 8B)\ntensor shape per layer: [batch, kv_heads, tokens, head_dim] for K and the same for V\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In Hugging Face transformers you can see this directly: after a forward pass with \u003ccode>use_cache=True\u003c/code>, the returned cache for a small model holds one key tensor per layer shaped like \u003ccode>(1, 3, 1250, 64)\u003c/code>: batch 1, 3 KV heads, 1,250 tokens, 64 numbers per head.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why caching is safe\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Token i's key and value at layer L depend only on tokens 1 to i, because the causal mask hid everything after i in every layer below. Appending token n+1 cannot change them. The flip side matters for prompt design: if you edit token 100, every key and value from position 100 onwards, in every layer, is invalid and must be recomputed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>With and without the cache\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Without a cache, producing each new token means running the model over the whole sequence again. With a cache, you run the model on one new token:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Prompt of 1,000 tokens, generate 500 tokens.\n\nWithout cache:  token positions processed = 1,000 + 1,001 + ... + 1,499 = 624,750\nWith cache:     1,000 (prefill, once) + 500 (one per decode step)    =   1,500\n\nAbout 400 times less work through the projections and MLPs.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Attention itself still compares the new query with every cached key, so each decode step's attention cost grows with context length, but linearly rather than as a full recomputation. On a small model on a CPU, generating 32 tokens after a 41-token prompt ran at 21 tokens per second without the cache and 71 with it, with identical greedy output; the gap widens as prompts get longer. You will measure this yourself in this module's build.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>It grows with every token\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Per-token size is fixed by the architecture:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>KV bytes per token = 2 (K and V) × layers × kv_heads × head_dim × bytes per value\n\nLlama 3.1 8B (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, 16-bit):\n2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 bytes = 128 KiB per token\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Context\u003c/th>\u003cth>KV cache for one conversation\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1,000 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>125 MiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>8,192 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 GiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>32,768 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4 GiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>131,072 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16 GiB (about the size of the model's own weights)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Multiply by the number of conversations being served at once. The next lesson turns this into GPU capacity planning.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Consequences you will feel\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Long conversations slow down\u003c/b> per token, because every decode step reads the whole cache.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Long contexts reduce concurrency\u003c/b> and cost providers more, which shows up as rate limits and long-context price tiers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Editing early context forces recomputation.\u003c/b> Changing the system prompt or an early message invalidates everything after the change (lesson 9).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The cache lives only as long as the server keeps it.\u003c/b> Normally it is freed when your request finishes; prefix caching keeps it around for later requests.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Serving engines allocate the cache in fixed-size blocks (16 tokens by default in vLLM's PagedAttention) so memory is not wasted on fragmentation; the Serving module covers this.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The cache can be stored at lower precision (8-bit halves it) and, in some systems, offloaded to CPU memory or SSD for later reuse.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When you see \"context window\", think \"how much KV cache per request the provider is willing to hold\".\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you estimate KV-cache memory for a long context? Work an example.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Multiply 2 (keys and values) × number of layers × number of KV heads × head dimension × bytes per value to get bytes per token, then multiply by the number of tokens and by the number of concurrent sequences. For Llama 3.1 8B (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head dimension 128, 16-bit values): 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 bytes, or 128 KiB per token; a 128,000-token (131,072) context therefore needs 16 GiB, about 17.2 GB, for one sequence, as much as the model's weights. An 8-bit cache halves that to 8 GiB.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why are queries not cached?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Only the newest token's query is used in a decode step: it is compared with the stored keys of all earlier tokens. Old queries are never needed again.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does editing the first message of a conversation force the server to recompute everything after it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every later token's keys and values were computed with attention over the earlier tokens, including the first message. Change it and all of those cached values are wrong, so the whole sequence from the edit onwards must be prefilled again.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Without a KV cache, roughly how much more work would generating 500 tokens after a 1,000-token prompt take?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 400 times more work through the projection and MLP layers: 624,750 token positions processed (1,000 + 1,001 + ... + 1,499) against 1,500 with the cache.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:inference/kv-cache-memory-maths":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>KV-cache memory is simple arithmetic: bytes per token (fixed by the architecture) × tokens × concurrent sequences. On a GPU, the memory left after the weights, divided by bytes per token, is the total number of tokens you can hold across all users at once. Long contexts therefore trade directly against concurrency, which is why context length, price and rate limits are linked.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The formula\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>KV bytes = 2 × layers × kv_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_value × tokens × sequences\n           ^ one K and one V\n\nFrom config.json:\n  layers    = num_hidden_layers\n  kv_heads  = num_key_value_heads     (if absent, equals num_attention_heads: plain MHA)\n  head_dim  = head_dim                (if absent, hidden_size ÷ num_attention_heads)\n  bytes     = 2 for a 16-bit cache (the usual default), 1 for an FP8 or 8-bit cache\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Watch the units. 1 KiB is 1,024 bytes and 1 GiB is 1,073,741,824 bytes, while GPU memory is quoted in GB (10^9 bytes). The 7% difference matters when you are close to the limit.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example 1: one conversation on Llama 3.1 8B\u003c/h4>\u003cp>From its config.json: 32 layers, 8 KV heads, hidden size 4,096 and 32 attention heads, so head_dim is 128.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>per token = 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 131,072 bytes = 128 KiB\n\n  8,192 tokens × 128 KiB =  1 GiB   (1.07 GB)\n 32,768 tokens × 128 KiB =  4 GiB   (4.3 GB)\n131,072 tokens × 128 KiB = 16 GiB   (17.2 GB)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example 2: how many users fit on one 80 GB GPU\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Serve Llama 3.1 8B in 16-bit precision on one 80 GB H100:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>usable memory      80 GB × 0.9 (vLLM's default gpu_memory_utilization)   = 72 GB\nminus weights      8.03 billion parameters × 2 bytes                       = 16.1 GB\nminus activations and runtime workspace (rough)                             ≈ 4 GB\nleft for the KV cache                                                       ≈ 52 GB\n\n52 GB ÷ 131,072 bytes per token ≈ 396,000 tokens in total, shared by all requests\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Context per request\u003c/th>\u003cth>Concurrent requests at full length\u003c/th>\u003cth>With an FP8 cache\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>8,192 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 48\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 96\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>32,768 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 12\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 24\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>131,072 tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 6\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>vLLM prints this calculation at start-up in a line like \u003ccode>GPU KV cache size: 396,000 tokens, Maximum concurrency for 131,072 tokens per request: 3.02x\u003c/code>. Real requests rarely fill the whole window, so actual concurrency is higher, but the total-token budget is the hard limit. When it runs out, the server queues new requests or pauses running ones and recomputes them later (\u003cb>preemption\u003c/b>), and latency jumps.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example 3: a 70B model\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Llama 3.1 70B: 80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>per token = 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 327,680 bytes = 320 KiB\none 131,072-token conversation = 40 GiB\n\nOn 4 × H100 (320 GB): 320 × 0.9 = 288 GB, minus 141 GB of 16-bit weights,\nminus about 8 GB of activations ≈ 139 GB for the KV cache ≈ 424,000 tokens\n-&gt; about 3 conversations at 128k tokens, or about 51 at 8k tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The weights alone fill two GPUs; long-context serving needs two more just for the cache.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What shrinks it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The levers, applied to Llama 3.1 8B at a 131,072-token context, with two other architectures for comparison:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Lever\u003c/th>\u003cth>KV per token\u003c/th>\u003cth>One 128k conversation\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Baseline: GQA with 8 KV heads, 16-bit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>128 KiB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16 GiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The same model if it used MHA (32 KV heads)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>512 KiB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>64 GiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>FP8 KV cache\u003c/td>\u003ctd>64 KiB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8 GiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4-bit cache (llama.cpp \u003ccode>q4_0\u003c/code>, about 4.5 bits with scales)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>36 KiB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.5 GiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>MLA: DeepSeek-V3, a 671B model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>69 KiB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8.6 GiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Alternating window and full layers: gpt-oss-120b\u003c/td>\u003ctd>36 KiB plus 4.5 MiB fixed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.5 GiB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Quantising the cache costs a little accuracy, more at 4 bits and at long contexts. Ollama's documentation, for example, describes its 8-bit cache as about half the memory with a very small loss in precision, and its 4-bit cache as about a quarter with a loss that may be more noticeable at higher context sizes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why long context limits concurrency and costs more\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The cache budget is shared. Memory that holds one 128,000-token conversation could hold sixteen 8,000-token ones. Long contexts also slow decode for everyone in the batch, because each step reads more cache, and their prefill is expensive. That is why providers cap context, charge more for very long prompts in some price tiers and enforce token-per-minute rate limits.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Do the arithmetic before choosing a model or a GPU for long contexts. Per-token KV size differs by more than ten times between models of similar parameter count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In vLLM, set \u003ccode>--max-model-len\u003c/code> to the longest context you actually need, consider \u003ccode>--kv-cache-dtype fp8\u003c/code>, and read the capacity line in the start-up log. In Ollama, the \u003ccode>num_ctx\u003c/code> option sets the context it reserves, and \u003ccode>OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE=q8_0\u003c/code> halves the cache.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Watch KV-cache utilisation and preemption counts in production; they are the early warning of a capacity problem.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def kv_bytes_per_token(cfg: dict, bytes_per_value: int = 2) -&gt; int:\n    layers = cfg[\"num_hidden_layers\"]\n    kv_heads = cfg.get(\"num_key_value_heads\", cfg[\"num_attention_heads\"])\n    head_dim = cfg.get(\"head_dim\") or cfg[\"hidden_size\"] // cfg[\"num_attention_heads\"]\n    return 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * bytes_per_value\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the per-token KV cache of Llama 3.1 70B in 16-bit, and how much does a 128,000-token conversation need?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 327,680 bytes, which is 320 KiB per token; 131,072 tokens need 40 GiB, about 43 GB.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">After loading Llama 3.1 8B you have 40 GB left for the KV cache. How many 32,768-token conversations fit at full length?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>40 × 10^9 ÷ 131,072 bytes ≈ 305,000 tokens in total; divided by 32,768 that is about 9 conversations.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does switching to an FP8 KV cache roughly double how many users you can serve?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each cached value takes 1 byte instead of 2, so bytes per token halve and twice as many tokens fit in the same memory. It also reduces the bytes read per decode step, which helps speed at long contexts, at the cost of a small accuracy loss.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do providers price or limit very long prompts differently?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A long conversation occupies a large share of the GPU's KV-cache budget for its whole duration, slows decode for other requests in the batch and reduces how many requests can be served, and its prefill is expensive. Long-context tiers and token rate limits pass that cost on.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Try it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Open Qwen3-8B's config.json and compute its per-token KV size and its size at 32,768 tokens, then check: 36 layers, 8 KV heads and head_dim 128 give 2 × 36 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 147,456 bytes (144 KiB) per token and 4.5 GiB at 32,768 tokens. Then do the same for a model you use, and work out how many full-length conversations fit on a 24 GB consumer GPU after its weights.\u003c/p>","c:sampling-temperature-top-p-and-greedy":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>At each step the model outputs one logit per vocabulary token, softmax turns them into probabilities and a \u003cb>sampler\u003c/b> picks one. \u003cb>Temperature\u003c/b> divides the logits before softmax (low sharpens, high flattens); \u003cb>top-k\u003c/b>, \u003cb>top-p\u003c/b> and \u003cb>min-p\u003c/b> cut off the unlikely tail; \u003cb>penalties\u003c/b> discourage repetition; a \u003cb>seed\u003c/b> fixes the random draw. Greedy decoding always takes the top token: deterministic in principle, but prone to dull or looping text.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Temperature, worked\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Suppose that after \"The weather today is\" four candidate tokens have these logits (a real model has a hundred thousand more, mostly negligible). Probabilities come from a softmax of the logits divided by the temperature T:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>p_i = exp(z_i / T) / sum over j of exp(z_j / T)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Token\u003c/th>\u003cth>Logit\u003c/th>\u003cth>T = 0.5\u003c/th>\u003cth>T = 1.0\u003c/th>\u003cth>T = 2.0\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>sunny\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2.0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.844\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.621\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.452\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>cloudy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.114\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.229\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.274\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>rainy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.042\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.139\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.213\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>purple\u003c/td>\u003ctd>-2.0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.011\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.061\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Dividing by T below 1 stretches the gaps between logits, so the favourite dominates; T above 1 shrinks them, so unlikely tokens such as \"purple\" get real chances. \u003cb>T = 0\u003c/b> is treated as greedy: always \"sunny\".\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Truncation: top-k, top-p and min-p\u003c/h4>\u003cp>These remove the tail before sampling, then renormalise. Using the T = 1.0 column:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Top-k = 2\u003c/b> keeps the two most likely tokens: sunny and cloudy, renormalised to 0.73 and 0.27.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Top-p = 0.9\u003c/b> (nucleus sampling) keeps the smallest set whose probabilities add up to at least 0.9: sunny (0.621), cloudy (0.850 cumulative) and rainy (0.989). Purple is dropped. When the model is confident the set is small; when it is unsure the set grows.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Min-p = 0.1\u003c/b> keeps tokens with at least 10% of the top token's probability. The threshold is 0.1 × 0.621 = 0.062, so sunny, cloudy and rainy stay and purple (0.011) goes. Because the threshold scales with the top probability, min-p stays coherent at high temperatures, which is what its 2024 paper reports.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import numpy as np\n\ndef sample(logits, temperature=1.0, top_k=0, top_p=1.0, min_p=0.0, rng=np.random.default_rng()):\n    if temperature == 0:\n        return int(np.argmax(logits))                      # greedy\n    z = np.asarray(logits, dtype=float) / temperature\n    p = np.exp(z - z.max()); p /= p.sum()                  # softmax\n    if top_k:\n        p[p &lt; np.sort(p)[-top_k]] = 0                      # keep the k largest\n    if min_p:\n        p[p &lt; min_p * p.max()] = 0                         # keep tokens close to the top one\n    if top_p &lt; 1.0:\n        order = np.argsort(p)[::-1]\n        mass_before = np.cumsum(p[order]) - p[order]\n        p[order[mass_before &gt;= top_p]] = 0                 # drop the tail beyond top_p\n    p /= p.sum()\n    return int(rng.choice(len(p), p=p))\n\nprint(sample([2.0, 1.0, 0.5, -2.0], temperature=1.0, top_p=0.9))   # 0, 1 or 2; never 3\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Penalties, seeds and other controls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Repetition penalty\u003c/b> (common in open-model servers): divides the positive logits of tokens already present by a factor such as 1.1, making repeats less likely.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Presence penalty\u003c/b> (OpenAI-style APIs): subtracts a fixed amount from the logit of any token that has already appeared, nudging towards new words and topics.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Frequency penalty\u003c/b>: subtracts an amount proportional to how often a token has appeared, reducing verbatim loops.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Seed\u003c/b>: fixes the random number generator so the same probabilities give the same draw. It does not remove numerical differences between runs (next lesson), so hosted APIs describe seeds as best effort.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Logit bias, stop sequences and maximum tokens\u003c/b>: force or ban specific tokens, end at a marker, cap the length.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Settings per use case\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Use case\u003c/th>\u003cth>Temperature\u003c/th>\u003cth>Truncation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Extraction, classification, JSON\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0 to 0.2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pair with structured outputs (lesson 11)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Code generation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0 to 0.4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Top-p 0.95\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Raise it when you sample several candidates and test them\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>General chat assistant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.5 to 0.8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Top-p 0.9 to 0.95\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider defaults are usually tuned for this\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Brainstorming, creative writing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.9 to 1.2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Min-p 0.05 to 0.1, or top-p 0.95\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Temperature for variety, truncation for coherence\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Self-consistency, best-of-n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.7 to 1.0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Top-p 0.95\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Diversity between samples is the point\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reasoning models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Vendor's recommendation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Usually fixed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Qwen3's model card recommends T 0.6, top-p 0.95 and top-k 20 in thinking mode and warns against greedy decoding\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Change one setting at a time and evaluate. OpenAI's API reference recommends adjusting temperature or top-p, not both.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Small models at low temperature are the most likely to loop; add a mild repetition or presence penalty, or raise the temperature slightly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Several hosted reasoning models fix or ignore sampling parameters because their training assumed particular settings; check the docs before tuning.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Log sampling parameters with every request so you can reproduce and compare behaviour.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">If you generate ten completions of the same prompt at temperature 0, 0.7 and 1.5, what differences do you expect, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At 0 the sampler takes the top token every time, so the ten completions are identical or nearly so (small numerical differences can still cause rare divergences). At 0.7 they share structure and most content but vary in wording, because likely alternatives are picked now and then. At 1.5 the distribution is flattened, so low-probability tokens are chosen often: completions differ a lot, and some drift off topic or contain errors and odd words, unless truncation such as min-p or top-p trims the tail.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Using the table, what happens to the probability of \"purple\" as the temperature rises from 0.5 to 2, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It rises from almost 0 to 0.011 at T = 1 and 0.061 at T = 2, because dividing the logits by a larger T shrinks the gap between purple's logit and the others before softmax.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">With probabilities 0.621, 0.229, 0.139 and 0.011, which tokens does top-p = 0.9 keep, and which does min-p = 0.1 keep?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Top-p 0.9 keeps the first three, because the running total only reaches 0.9 once the third is included (0.989). Min-p 0.1 sets a threshold of 0.062 and also keeps the first three, dropping the 0.011 token.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does greedy decoding often produce repetitive loops?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Once a phrase has appeared, repeating it often becomes the single most likely continuation, and greedy decoding always takes the most likely token, so the model can lock into a cycle. Sampling, truncation and repetition penalties break the cycle.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:inference/determinism-why-temperature-0-is-not-reproducibl":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Temperature 0 makes the sampler deterministic, but the logits it reads can differ very slightly between runs. Floating-point addition is not associative, and inference kernels add numbers in an order that depends on how many requests share the batch, which depends on other users' traffic. When two candidate tokens are nearly tied, a tiny difference flips the choice and the rest of the output diverges. Treat model outputs as samples from a distribution: test with tolerances, not exact string matches.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Floating point is not associative\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>&gt;&gt;&gt; (0.1 + 1e20) - 1e20\n0.0\n&gt;&gt;&gt; 0.1 + (1e20 - 1e20)\n0.1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Computers store numbers with limited precision, so adding a small number to a large one can lose the small one entirely, and the order of additions changes the result. In 16-bit formats, with only about three significant decimal digits, the effect is far larger than in this 64-bit example. A transformer does billions of additions per token, so any change in their order shifts the logits in their last digits.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where the changing order comes from\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A common explanation is \"GPU threads finish in a random order\". Researchers at Thinking Machines showed in 2025 that this is mostly not the cause: running the same matrix multiplication on the same data repeatedly gives bit-identical results. The main culprit is a lack of \u003cb>batch invariance\u003c/b>:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Batch size changes with load.\u003c/b> With continuous batching (lesson 8), your request shares each forward pass with however many other requests are running. Kernels for matrix multiplication, normalisation and attention pick different strategies for splitting and summing the work at different batch sizes, so the numbers for your row are added in a different order. You cannot control who else is in the batch, so from your side the server is non-deterministic.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Different code paths.\u003c/b> Prefill versus decode, chunked prefill, and a prefix-cache hit versus a miss can compute the same token's attention with different splits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Different hardware and software.\u003c/b> A provider's fleet mixes GPU types, parallelism layouts and library versions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mixture-of-experts routing.\u003c/b> A tiny difference can tip a token to a different expert, a large discrete change; some MoE implementations also cap tokens per expert per batch, so other users' tokens can affect yours.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Silent updates\u003c/b> to models or infrastructure on the provider's side.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>How big is the effect?\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In the Thinking Machines experiment, Qwen3-235B generated 1,000 completions of \"Tell me about Richard Feynman\" at temperature 0, 1,000 tokens each, on vLLM. There were \u003cb>80 different completions\u003c/b>. All were identical for the first 102 tokens; at token 103, 992 continued with \"Queens, New York\" and 8 with \"New York City\", and the texts differed from there on. With batch-invariant kernels all 1,000 were identical. The price was speed: in their test the unoptimised deterministic mode took 55 seconds against 26 for default vLLM, and 42 seconds with an improved attention kernel.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The pattern is typical: outputs agree for a while, then one near-tie flips and the continuations split.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What to do about it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Test meaning, not strings.\u003c/b> Parse JSON and check fields, compare extracted values, and use graders or rubric-based judges for free text (Evals module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Measure rates.\u003c/b> Run important eval cases several times and report pass rates. A case that passes 7 times out of 10 is a different problem from one that always fails.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pin and log.\u003c/b> Use dated model snapshots, and log the model version, sampling parameters and any system fingerprint the provider returns.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Use seeds as a help, not a guarantee.\u003c/b> Where an API accepts a seed, it makes the sampling step repeatable but cannot remove numerical drift. OpenAI's Chat Completions reference calls its \u003ccode>seed\u003c/code> parameter best effort and, as of late 2026, marks it deprecated.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Need exact repeats? Cache the response.\u003c/b> When identical inputs must give identical outputs (audits, demos), store the first response and replay it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Self-hosting?\u003c/b> vLLM's batch-invariant mode (\u003ccode>VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT=1\u003c/code>, in beta as of late 2026) and similar options in SGLang give reproducible outputs at a throughput cost. Reinforcement-learning pipelines and regression tests are the usual users.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can the same prompt at temperature 0 give two different outputs from an API?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The sampler is deterministic, but the logits are not bit-identical between runs: kernels sum numbers in an order that depends on batch size and code path, which depend on server load, and floating-point addition is not associative. When the top two tokens are nearly tied, the tiny difference can change which one is chosen, and the outputs diverge from there.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do divergent outputs usually agree for a while and then differ completely?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Most tokens have a clear winner, so small numerical noise does not change them. At the first near-tie the choice can flip, and every later token is conditioned on a different prefix, so the continuations go separate ways.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How should a regression test for an LLM feature compare outputs?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>On meaning: parse structured output and assert on fields and values, or use a grader for free text, and run each case several times to measure a pass rate against a threshold rather than comparing exact strings.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does batch invariance mean?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>That a request's numerical results do not depend on how many other requests are processed alongside it or in what order, which requires every kernel to use the same reduction order for each row regardless of batch size.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:inference/latency-anatomy":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>End-to-end latency is \u003cb>time to first token (TTFT)\u003c/b> plus \u003cb>time per output token (TPOT, also called inter-token latency)\u003c/b> times the number of output tokens. TTFT is network, queueing and prefill, so it grows with uncached prompt length and server load. TPOT is decode speed, set by model size, hardware, batch load and context length. Users feel TTFT as responsiveness and TPOT as speed; for long answers, output length dominates everything.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The numbers\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>total latency ≈ TTFT + TPOT × (output tokens − 1)\n\nTTFT  = network round trip + queueing + prefill of uncached prompt tokens (+ hidden reasoning, see below)\nTPOT  = time between streamed tokens        tokens per second per user = 1 ÷ TPOT\nthroughput = output tokens per second across all users of a server\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For scale: a TPOT of 100 ms is 10 tokens per second, about 450 words per minute, already faster than most people read; a TPOT of 12.5 ms is 80 tokens per second.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Simple example: TTFT 0.4 s and 500 output tokens at 50 tokens per second (TPOT 20 ms) give 0.4 + 499 × 0.02 ≈ 10.4 seconds. Halving the answer length saves 5 seconds; halving TTFT saves 0.2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Reasoning models change the picture\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A reasoning model generates hidden reasoning tokens before the visible answer. They are decode tokens, so they add to the time to the first \u003ci>visible\u003c/i> token. An illustrative request: 80 ms network, 50 ms queue, 100 ms prefill, then 1,200 reasoning tokens and a 400-token answer at 15 ms per token:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>time to first visible token = 0.08 + 0.05 + 0.10 + 1,200 × 0.015 ≈ 18.2 s\ntotal                       ≈ 18.2 + 399 × 0.015                ≈ 24.2 s\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>High tokens per second does not make it feel fast; lower reasoning effort does.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What drives each part\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Driver\u003c/th>\u003cth>Effect on TTFT\u003c/th>\u003cth>Effect on TPOT\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Longer uncached prompt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up, roughly in proportion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Slightly up (bigger cache to read)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Prompt cache hit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Strongly down\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Bigger model (more active parameters)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Busy server, large batches\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up (queueing)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Long conversation so far\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up if not cached\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up (each step reads more KV cache)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hidden reasoning tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up (before the visible answer)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Distant region, slow network\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Slight\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>First request with a new output schema\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can be up while the grammar is compiled\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Slight\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>More output tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Total time grows in proportion\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Measure it yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With any OpenAI-compatible endpoint, stream the response and time it:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()  # or OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\") for Ollama\n\ndef measure(messages, model=\"&lt;model&gt;\"):\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    first = last = usage = None\n    stream = client.chat.completions.create(model=model, messages=messages, stream=True,\n                                            stream_options={\"include_usage\": True})\n    for chunk in stream:\n        if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n            last = time.perf_counter()\n            first = first or last\n        if chunk.usage:\n            usage = chunk.usage\n    n = usage.completion_tokens\n    return {\"ttft_s\": first - t0, \"total_s\": last - t0,\n            \"tpot_ms\": 1000 * (last - first) / max(n - 1, 1), \"output_tokens\": n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Streamed chunks do not map one to one onto tokens, so take the token count from the final usage chunk. For reasoning models, \u003ccode>completion_tokens\u003c/code> includes hidden reasoning tokens, so compute TPOT from visible tokens only or treat the figure as approximate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Report percentiles.\u003c/b> p50 tells you the typical experience, p95 and p99 the complaints. Measure at realistic concurrency, not on an idle endpoint.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The levers, roughly in order of impact:\u003c/b> generate fewer tokens (concise formats, lower reasoning effort); use a smaller or faster model for the step; stream; cache prompt prefixes; make independent calls in parallel; move closer to the provider's region; pay for priority processing where offered.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Goodput\u003c/b> (requests per second that meet your latency targets) is a better capacity metric than raw throughput.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A response has a TTFT of 0.4 seconds and streams 500 tokens at 50 tokens per second. What is the total latency?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 0.4 + 499 × 0.02 = 10.4 seconds.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">TTFT is high but tokens stream quickly once they start. Name three likely causes.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A long uncached prompt (prefill time), queueing on a busy server, hidden reasoning tokens before the visible answer, a slow network or distant region, or a new structured-output schema being compiled.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a reasoning model feel slow even when its tokens per second are high?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It generates hundreds or thousands of hidden reasoning tokens before the first visible token, so the time to the first visible token is long regardless of decode speed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which single change usually cuts total latency most for long answers?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Generating fewer output tokens, through a more concise format, a lower reasoning effort or a tighter length limit, because total time grows in proportion to output tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:inference/batching-serving-many-users-at-once":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Because decode is memory-bound, serving one sequence at a time wastes most of a GPU: the weights read for one token could serve hundreds. Servers therefore put many users' sequences into each forward pass. \u003cb>Static batching\u003c/b> waits until every sequence in a batch has finished; \u003cb>continuous batching\u003c/b> swaps finished sequences out and new ones in at every step, keeping the GPU full. It is why API prices can be low, and why your latency depends on other people's traffic.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why batching is nearly free for decode\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each decode step costs roughly: read all the weights once (shared by the whole batch), plus read each sequence's KV cache, plus a little arithmetic. For Llama 3.1 8B on one H100 (16.1 GB of weights, 3.35 TB/s), with every sequence holding 2,048 tokens of context (256 MiB of cache each):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Batch size\u003c/th>\u003cth>Bytes read per step\u003c/th>\u003cth>Step time (bandwidth limit)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tokens per second, all users\u003c/th>\u003cth>Per user\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16.1 GB + 0.27 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4.9 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 205\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 205\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>64\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16.1 GB + 17.2 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>9.9 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 6,450\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 100\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Throughput rises about 30 times while each user's speed halves. The arithmetic for 64 tokens takes about 1 ms on this GPU, far below the 9.9 ms of memory reads, so the step is still memory-bound. Push the batch higher and eventually KV reads dominate, memory runs out or compute becomes the limit. Choosing a batch size is choosing a point on the curve between throughput and per-user speed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Static versus continuous batching\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Requests finish at different times because their outputs have different lengths.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Static batching: 4 slots; a new batch starts only when all four have finished\nslot 1:  A A A A A A A A A A A A\nslot 2:  B B B B . . . . . . . .      . = slot idle\nslot 3:  C C C C C C C . . . . .\nslot 4:  D D . . . . . . . . . .\n         requests E, F, G and H wait in the queue\n\nContinuous batching: a finished slot is refilled at the next step\nslot 1:  A A A A A A A A A A A A\nslot 2:  B B B B E E E E E E E E\nslot 3:  C C C C C C C F F F F F\nslot 4:  D D G G G G G G G G H H\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Continuous batching\u003c/b> (also called iteration-level scheduling, introduced by the Orca system in 2022) makes scheduling decisions at every decode step: a new request's prefill is slotted in, its decode joins the running batch, and finished sequences leave at once. Anyscale's 2023 benchmark measured about 8 times the throughput of naive static batching from continuous batching alone, and up to 23 times combined with vLLM's memory management.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two companions make it work:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Paged KV memory.\u003c/b> vLLM's PagedAttention allocates the cache in small fixed blocks, like pages in an operating system, instead of one contiguous region sized for the maximum length. Almost no memory is wasted, so more sequences fit in a batch; the vLLM paper reports 2 to 4 times the throughput of earlier systems at the same latency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Chunked prefill.\u003c/b> A long new prompt is split into chunks processed alongside ongoing decode steps, so one 50,000-token prompt does not freeze everyone else's stream.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>What it means for you\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>As an API user\u003c/b>: your time to first token and token rate depend on how loaded the provider's servers are, one reason latency varies by time of day. Priority tiers buy a better place in the queue; flex and batch tiers (batch is about half price as of late 2026) let the provider fit your work into spare capacity.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>As a self-hoster\u003c/b>: the maximum number of concurrent sequences (\u003ccode>max_num_seqs\u003c/code> in vLLM) and the KV budget set your throughput and per-user speed. Benchmark at the concurrency you expect, never with a single request.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Long requests are expensive neighbours\u003c/b>: a conversation with a huge context takes KV memory and bandwidth from everyone else in the batch.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does adding sequences to a decode batch barely slow each user down, at first?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Decode is memory-bound: each step's time is dominated by reading the weights, which happens once for the whole batch. Extra sequences add only their own KV-cache reads and a little arithmetic, so total throughput rises almost in proportion while per-user speed falls slowly, until KV reads, memory capacity or compute become the bottleneck.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What problem with static batching does continuous batching solve?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In static batching, sequences that finish early leave their slots idle until the longest one finishes, and new requests wait for the whole batch. Continuous batching refills a slot as soon as its sequence finishes, at every step, keeping the GPU busy and cutting queueing time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does one long-context request reduce how many others can be batched?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Its KV cache takes a large share of the fixed KV memory budget (16 GiB at 128,000 tokens for an 8B model), leaving less for other sequences, and it adds a large cache read to every decode step.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do servers use chunked prefill?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A long prompt processed in one go would occupy the GPU for its whole prefill and stall every other user's decode. Splitting it into chunks interleaved with decode steps keeps everyone's streams flowing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:inference/prefix-caching-reusing-kv-across-requests":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Many requests start with the same tokens: a system prompt, tool definitions, a shared document, the earlier turns of a conversation. A server that keeps the KV cache of those tokens can skip their prefill next time, cutting time to first token and compute. Because each token's keys and values depend on every token before it, reuse works only for an identical prefix: change one token and everything after it must be recomputed. Provider \"prompt caching\" is this mechanism with a price attached.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why only prefixes\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Token i's keys and values in a layer were computed by attending over tokens 1 to i in the layers below. The same word at the same position after a different earlier token has different keys and values. So a cached entry is valid only for \u003ci>the exact sequence of tokens up to and including it\u003c/i>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Request 1:  [system 2,000][tools 1,500][user: \"Where is my order?\"]\nRequest 2:  [system 2,000][tools 1,500][user: \"Cancel my subscription\"]\n            -&gt; 3,500 shared tokens can be reused; only the new question is prefilled\n\nRequest 3:  [time 2026-10-28 10:41:07][system 2,000][tools 1,500][user ...]\n            -&gt; the first tokens differ on every request, so nothing is reusable\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Block hashing: how vLLM does it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>vLLM divides the token sequence into fixed blocks (16 tokens by default) and gives each full block a hash computed from the \u003cb>parent block's hash\u003c/b> plus the block's own tokens (plus extras such as an adapter ID, image hashes or a tenant salt). Because each hash includes its parent's, a block's hash identifies the entire prefix up to its end. For a new request the server hashes the prompt block by block and looks the hashes up in order until the first miss; every block before the miss is reused.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import hashlib\n\ndef block_hashes(tokens, block=16, salt=\"\"):\n    hashes, parent = [], salt\n    for start in range(0, len(tokens) - len(tokens) % block, block):   # full blocks only\n        parent = hashlib.sha256(f\"{parent}|{tokens[start:start + block]}\".encode()).hexdigest()\n        hashes.append(parent)\n    return hashes\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A worked example, run with that function: request 1 has a 3,500-token shared prefix plus 30 tokens of its own (3,530 tokens). Request 2 has the same prefix and a different 30-token question.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Request 1:  3,530 prompt tokens:     0 reused, 3,530 prefilled  (cold cache)\nRequest 2:  3,530 prompt tokens: 3,488 reused,    42 prefilled\n            3,488 = 218 full blocks of 16. Block 219 covers tokens 3,489 to 3,504 and\n            contains the first differing token (3,501), so it and everything after it are recomputed.\nRequest 3:  one extra token added at the very start: 0 reused\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Cached blocks are reference-counted. When no running request uses a block it is not deleted; it joins a free queue in least-recently-used order and stays reusable until its memory is needed. Popular prefixes stay warm and rare ones are evicted.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Radix trees: how SGLang does it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>SGLang's \u003cb>RadixAttention\u003c/b> keeps cached sequences in a radix tree, a prefix tree whose edges hold runs of tokens. A new request walks down the tree to find its longest matching prefix; branches represent different continuations of a shared start, and least-recently-used leaves are evicted first.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>root\n└── [system prompt + tools: 3,500 tokens]\n     ├── [user A, turn 1][assistant][user A, turn 2] ...\n     ├── [user B, turn 1][assistant] ...\n     └── [few-shot examples][user C] ...\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The SGLang paper reports up to 6.4 times higher throughput than earlier systems on workloads such as agents, few-shot prompts and multi-turn chat.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>From engine to provider pricing\u003c/h4>\u003cp>OpenAI, Anthropic and Google run the same mechanism at scale and expose it as \u003cb>prompt caching\u003c/b> (the Billing module covers prices and each provider's rules). Each rule follows from the mechanism:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exact prefix only\u003c/b>: one changed early token, or a reordered tool list, means a miss from that point on.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A minimum length\u003c/b> (1,024 tokens on recent OpenAI models, as of late 2026): tiny prefixes are not worth the bookkeeping, and caching works in blocks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Short lifetimes\u003c/b> (minutes by default, longer for a fee): cached blocks occupy memory that other traffic wants.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Routing\u003c/b>: your request must reach a machine that holds the cache. OpenAI documents that it routes by a hash of the initial prompt tokens, and older models accept a \u003ccode>prompt_cache_key\u003c/code> to keep related requests together.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cheaper reads\u003c/b>: cached input tokens are billed at a small fraction of the normal input price, because their prefill is skipped; some providers charge extra the first time a prefix is written.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Designing for hits\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Order prompts from \u003cb>stable to volatile\u003c/b>: tool definitions, system prompt, long reference documents, conversation history, then the new message.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep history \u003cb>append-only\u003c/b>. Editing or summarising an early turn invalidates everything after it (Memory module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Never put timestamps, request IDs or per-user details near the top; put them at the end if they are needed at all.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Serialise tool schemas and JSON deterministically, with a fixed key order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In multi-tenant self-hosted systems, isolate caches per tenant (vLLM accepts a \u003ccode>cache_salt\u003c/code>), because a faster response could otherwise reveal, through timing, that another tenant sent the same text.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does changing the token at position 100 invalidate the cache from position 100 onwards but not positions 1 to 99?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keys and values at each position depend only on that token and the ones before it. Positions 1 to 99 never saw the changed token, but every position from 100 onwards attended to it, so their cached values no longer match.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">With 16-token blocks, two requests share their first 1,000 tokens and then differ. How many tokens can the second request reuse?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>992: the 62 full blocks (62 × 16 = 992) before the block covering tokens 993 to 1,008, which contains the first differing token (1,001) and so cannot be reused.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A team puts the current time at the top of the system prompt and its cache hit rate is zero. Why, and what is the fix?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The time changes on every request, so the very first tokens differ and no prefix ever matches. Move the timestamp to the end of the prompt or into the latest user message, or round it so that it rarely changes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can two identical requests sent a few minutes apart still miss the cache?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The cached blocks may have been evicted under memory pressure or expired, the second request may have been routed to a different machine, or the prefix may be shorter than the provider's minimum cacheable length.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:inference/context-windows-in-practice":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The \u003cb>context window\u003c/b> is the maximum number of tokens a model handles in one request, counting the input and everything it generates, including hidden reasoning. Output also has its own, smaller cap. Hitting either limit causes an error or a truncated answer. Quality, however, starts to degrade long before the limit: the \u003cb>effective context\u003c/b> is shorter than the advertised one. Budget the window deliberately.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What counts\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Everything you send and everything the model generates in that request:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>the system prompt and developer instructions\u003c/li>\u003cli>tool definitions, rendered into the prompt by the chat template\u003c/li>\u003cli>every message in the history, including tool calls and tool results\u003c/li>\u003cli>images, PDF pages and audio, converted to tokens\u003c/li>\u003cli>the output, including reasoning or thinking tokens\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>As of late 2026, frontier APIs commonly offer windows from a few hundred thousand to one million tokens with separate output caps; Anthropic's current models, for example, have a 1M-token window and allow up to 128,000 output tokens per request. Open models are typically 128,000 to 256,000 tokens.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Hard limits and what happens at them\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Input too long\u003c/b>: the API rejects the request with an error, or a chat product quietly drops the oldest turns.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Output reaches the maximum\u003c/b>: generation stops with a \u003ccode>length\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code> stop reason and the answer is cut off.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Input plus requested output exceeds the window\u003c/b>: depending on the API, the request is rejected or the output allowance shrinks; check your provider's docs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>A budget for one request to a model with a 200,000-token window:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Part\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tokens\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>System prompt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool definitions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieved documents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>40,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Conversation history\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>New user message\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Input total\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>79,000\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reserved for reasoning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reserved for the answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Request total\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>127,000\u003c/b>, leaving 73,000 of headroom\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why the limit exists\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The window is set by the lengths the model was trained and scaled for (RoPE extension, Inside an LLM module), by KV-cache memory per request (16 GiB at 128,000 tokens even for an 8B model) and by prefill compute, which grows faster than linearly at long lengths. Longer prompts also take longer to process and, without caching, cost more on every turn.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Effective context and context rot\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Models can accept a long input yet use it badly:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Lost in the middle\u003c/b> (2023): on multi-document question answering, accuracy was highest when the relevant passage was at the start or end of the context and dropped significantly when it sat in the middle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>RULER\u003c/b> (2024): of 17 models that all claimed at least 32,000 tokens, only half kept satisfactory performance at 32,000 once tasks went beyond simple retrieval.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Context rot\u003c/b> (Chroma, 2025): across 18 models, performance on deliberately simple tasks degraded as input length grew, in non-uniform ways, and distracting, similar-looking content made it worse. Needle-in-a-haystack tests miss this because they only require finding an exact phrase.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Anthropic's documentation now names this context rot and says that curating what goes into the window matters as much as how much space is available.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keep context lean\u003c/b>: retrieve the relevant passages instead of pasting whole documents, and drop stale tool results (Context and Memory modules).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Place what matters well\u003c/b>: instructions at the start, and the question at the end after long documents.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Count before you send\u003c/b>: use the tokeniser or the provider's token-counting endpoint, and reserve explicit room for reasoning and output.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Test at your real lengths\u003c/b>: measure accuracy on your own task at the context sizes you will actually use, not at the advertised maximum.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Do output and reasoning tokens count toward the context window?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Yes. The window covers the whole request: all input plus everything generated, including hidden reasoning tokens. Output also has its own per-request maximum.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between a model's advertised context window and its effective context?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The advertised window is the hard limit the API accepts. The effective context is the length up to which the model still uses the information reliably on realistic tasks, which studies such as RULER and context rot show is often much shorter.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your assistant answers well with 10 retrieved passages but worse with 50, although both fit easily. What is happening, and what do you do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The extra passages dilute attention and add distractors, so the relevant information is used less reliably (context rot, lost in the middle). Retrieve fewer, better-ranked passages, put the most relevant first or last, and measure quality at each setting.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:inference/constrained-decoding-how-structured-outputs-work":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A model can be forced to produce valid output by \u003cb>constrained decoding\u003c/b>: before each token is sampled, every token that would break a grammar (a JSON Schema, a regular expression, a tool-call format) has its logit set to minus infinity. A grammar engine tracks where the output is in the grammar and computes the allowed set at every step. The result is guaranteed syntax, not guaranteed truth.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The mechanism\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Logits for whole vocabulary\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Grammar state\u003c/b>\u003csmall>what may come next?\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Mask\u003c/b>\u003csmall>invalid tokens set to minus infinity\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Sample from the rest\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Advance grammar state\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Example: the schema says the output is \u003ccode>{\"answer\": \"yes\"}\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>{\"answer\": \"no\"}\u003c/code>. Once the model has produced \u003ccode>{\"answer\": \"\u003c/code>, the grammar allows only tokens that continue towards \u003ccode>yes\"\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>no\"\u003c/code>, pieces such as \u003ccode>y\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>yes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>no\u003c/code>. Every other token in a vocabulary of 150,000 is masked. The model's own probabilities still decide between yes and no.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import numpy as np\n\ndef constrained_step(logits, allowed_ids):\n    masked = np.full_like(logits, -np.inf)\n    masked[allowed_ids] = logits[allowed_ids]      # keep only grammar-valid tokens\n    return int(np.argmax(masked))                  # or sample from softmax(masked)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The hard part is computing \u003ccode>allowed_ids\u003c/code> quickly at every step for a vocabulary of 100,000 to 200,000 multi-character tokens:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Outlines\u003c/b> (2023) compiles a regular expression or JSON Schema into a finite-state machine and precomputes, for each state, which vocabulary tokens are valid.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>XGrammar\u003c/b> (2024) handles full context-free grammars (nested JSON needs a stack) by splitting tokens into context-independent ones, checked ahead of time, and context-dependent ones, checked at run time, and it overlaps grammar work on the CPU with the GPU's forward pass. Its paper reports up to 100 times the speed of earlier solutions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>llguidance\u003c/b>, from Microsoft's Guidance project, computes a mask in about 50 microseconds of CPU time per token for a 128,000-token vocabulary. It is integrated into vLLM, SGLang and llama.cpp, and OpenAI announced in 2025 that it uses it for JSON Schema outputs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>As of late 2026, vLLM uses XGrammar or llguidance as its structured-output back ends.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>JSON mode versus structured outputs\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Feature\u003c/th>\u003cth>Guarantees\u003c/th>\u003cth>Does not guarantee\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>JSON mode\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Syntactically valid JSON\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your fields, types or required keys\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Structured outputs (schema-constrained)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Output matches the JSON Schema you supplied\u003c/td>\u003ctd>That the values are correct or complete\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Strict tool calling\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tool arguments match each tool's schema\u003c/td>\u003ctd>That the right tool was chosen\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>OpenAI describes structured outputs as the evolution of JSON mode and recommends them where available; Anthropic's structured outputs and strict tool use also rely on grammar-constrained decoding. Providers support only a subset of JSON Schema (strict modes typically require every property to be listed as required and disallow extra properties), so check the limitations.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Effects on speed and quality\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Speed\u003c/b>: with modern engines the per-token overhead is small. The first request with a new schema can be slower while the grammar is compiled and cached; OpenAI's docs warn of extra latency on the first request with a new schema in some cases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Quality can drop if the format fights the model.\u003c/b> Forcing the answer field first removes the model's chance to reason in tokens before committing; put a \u003ccode>reasoning\u003c/code> field first, or let a reasoning model think before the structured answer. Unusual formats the model rarely saw in training can also lower accuracy.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Token boundaries\u003c/b>: masking works on tokens, not characters, so a grammar can force token splits the model would not choose naturally, which slightly lowers quality. Good engines limit this, but it is one more reason simple schemas work best.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>It does not prevent truncation or refusals.\u003c/b> If generation hits the token limit the JSON is still cut off, and a refusal may come back in a separate field. Check the stop reason.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use schema-constrained outputs for anything a program parses: extraction, classification, tool arguments.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep schemas small and flat, enumerate allowed values, and describe fields clearly; descriptions guide the model's choices within the grammar.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Validate anyway (for example with Pydantic) and still evaluate the \u003ci>content\u003c/i>: a perfectly shaped object can hold a wrong value (Tools module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How does constrained decoding guarantee valid JSON?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At every step a grammar engine works out which vocabulary tokens can legally come next given the output so far, and the server sets the logits of all other tokens to minus infinity before sampling. The model can only choose among valid continuations, so the finished output always parses, unless generation is cut off by the token limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between JSON mode and structured outputs?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>JSON mode only guarantees syntactically valid JSON. Structured outputs constrain decoding to a specific JSON Schema, so required fields, types and allowed values are guaranteed as well.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can forcing a schema reduce answer quality, and how do you mitigate it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The constraint can remove the model's chance to reason before committing to an answer, and unusual formats or forced token splits move it away from patterns it learnt. Put a reasoning field before the answer field, or let a reasoning model think first; keep schemas simple; and measure quality with and without the constraint.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A structured-output response fails to parse. What is the most likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Generation hit the maximum token limit and was truncated (stop reason \u003ccode>length\u003c/code>), or the model refused. Constrained decoding guarantees the grammar only if generation runs to the end.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:inference/measure-the-kv-cache-and-latency-on-your-own-mac":"\u003cp>You will check this module's arithmetic against a real model: predict the KV cache from config.json, watch memory change with the context size, time prefill and decode, and see a prefix cache hit.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Ollama installed and running, with one small instruct model pulled: \u003ccode>ollama pull &lt;model&gt;\u003c/code> (any 1B to 8B instruct model; note which Hugging Face repository it comes from so you can read its config.json)\u003c/li>\u003cli>Python 3.11 or later; the script below uses only the standard library\u003c/li>\u003cli>No Ollama? Use the Hugging Face timing code from this module's build with a model under 1B parameters instead; steps 3 to 5 work the same way\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Predict.\u003c/b> Open the model's config.json on Hugging Face, compute KV bytes per token with \u003ccode>kv_bytes_per_token\u003c/code> from the KV-cache maths lesson (16-bit, Ollama's default), and predict the cache size at 4,096 and 32,768 tokens of context.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Watch memory change with the context size.\u003c/b> Load the model with two context sizes and compare the SIZE column:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>curl -s http://localhost:11434/api/generate -d '{\"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\", \"prompt\": \"hi\", \"stream\": false, \"options\": {\"num_ctx\": 4096}}' &gt; /dev/null\nollama ps\ncurl -s http://localhost:11434/api/generate -d '{\"model\": \"&lt;model&gt;\", \"prompt\": \"hi\", \"stream\": false, \"options\": {\"num_ctx\": 32768}}' &gt; /dev/null\nollama ps\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The increase should be close to your per-token figure × 28,672 extra tokens, plus some compute buffers. If it is a multiple of your prediction, check \u003ccode>OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL\u003c/code>: Ollama reserves the context for each parallel slot.\u003c/p>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Time prefill and decode against prompt length.\u003c/b> Each prompt starts with a random ID so the prefix cache cannot help:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json, time, uuid, urllib.request\n\nURL = \"http://localhost:11434/api/generate\"\nMODEL = \"&lt;model&gt;\"\nFILLER = \"The committee reviewed the quarterly figures and agreed to revisit the budget next month. \"\n\ndef generate(prompt, num_ctx=32768, num_predict=64):\n    body = {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": prompt, \"stream\": False, \"keep_alive\": \"10m\",\n            \"options\": {\"num_ctx\": num_ctx, \"num_predict\": num_predict, \"temperature\": 0}}\n    req = urllib.request.Request(URL, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    return json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(req))\n\ngenerate(\"Say hello.\")   # warm-up, so model loading is not measured\n\nprint(\"prompt  cached  prefill_ms  prefill_tok/s  decode_tok/s\")\nfor repeats in [10, 40, 160, 640]:\n    prompt = f\"Request {uuid.uuid4()}.\\n\" + FILLER * repeats + \"\\nSummarise the text above in one sentence.\"\n    r = generate(prompt)\n    cached = r.get(\"prompt_eval_cached_count\", 0)\n    prefill_s = r[\"prompt_eval_duration\"] / 1e9\n    decode_tps = r[\"eval_count\"] / (r[\"eval_duration\"] / 1e9)\n    print(f'{r[\"prompt_eval_count\"]:&gt;6}  {cached:&gt;6}  {prefill_s * 1000:&gt;10.0f}  '\n          f'{(r[\"prompt_eval_count\"] - cached) / prefill_s:&gt;13.0f}  {decode_tps:&gt;12.1f}')\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Add 1,280 repeats (about 22,000 tokens) if your machine is fast. \u003ccode>prompt_eval_duration\u003c/code> is effectively your TTFT on the server, without network time.\u003c/p>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>\u003cb>See the prefix cache.\u003c/b> Send one long prompt twice, then change only its first character, then append a question to the end:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>base = f\"Request {uuid.uuid4()}.\\n\" + FILLER * 640\nfor label, prompt in [(\"first\", base + \"\\nSummarise.\"), (\"repeat\", base + \"\\nSummarise.\"),\n                      (\"first char changed\", \"X\" + base[1:] + \"\\nSummarise.\"),\n                      (\"new question at end\", base + \"\\nList three key points.\")]:\n    r = generate(prompt)\n    print(f'{label:22s} prompt={r[\"prompt_eval_count\"]:&gt;6} cached={r.get(\"prompt_eval_cached_count\", \"n/a\")} '\n          f'prefill_ms={r[\"prompt_eval_duration\"] / 1e6:.0f}')\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Halve the cache.\u003c/b> Stop Ollama and restart it with an 8-bit cache, then repeat step 2:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION=1 OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE=q8_0 ollama serve\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Write up.\u003c/b> Put your predictions and measurements in one table: KV size per context, prefill tokens per second, decode tokens per second, and the expected decode ceiling (your machine's memory bandwidth ÷ the model file size).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Prefill runs at tens to hundreds of times the decode speed in tokens per second, because it processes the whole prompt in parallel.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Prefill time grows roughly in proportion to prompt length, a little faster at the longest prompts as attention's share grows; decode speed drops slightly as the context grows.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The repeated prompt reports almost all tokens cached and a prefill time near zero. Changing the first character makes everything uncached again; a new question at the end reuses the shared prefix and prefills only the new part.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The SIZE change between context lengths is close to your prediction, and q8_0 roughly halves the cache part.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why did changing only the first character of the prompt remove the cache benefit, while changing the last question did not?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every token's keys and values depend on all the tokens before it. A change at the start alters every position after it, so no cached block matches; a change at the end leaves the whole shared prefix identical, so only the new tokens need prefilling.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your decode speed is well below memory bandwidth ÷ model size. Name two likely reasons.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Part of the model may be running on the CPU because it did not fit in GPU memory (check the PROCESSOR column of \u003ccode>ollama ps\u003c/code>); the KV cache adds reads at long contexts; real kernels reach only part of peak bandwidth; or other processes are using the same memory bandwidth.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:token-and-sampling-lab":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Write the generation loop that sits inside every inference server, using Hugging Face transformers and a small open model that runs on a laptop CPU. You will count tokens across tokenisers, generate text naively (re-running the whole sequence for every token), then with a KV cache (prefill once, decode one token at a time), and add your own sampler. Then measure: how much faster the cache is at different prompt lengths, how big the cache is compared with the formula, and how sampling settings change the distribution of outputs. This is a standalone build, not part of Atlas, but the measurements feed your intuition for the Billing and Serving modules.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A 100M to 1B-parameter instruct model from Hugging Face (small SmolLM2 or Qwen models work well on a CPU), loaded with \u003ccode>AutoModelForCausalLM\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>Your own loop that calls \u003ccode>model(...)\u003c/code> directly; \u003ccode>model.generate()\u003c/code> only as a correctness check\u003c/li>\u003cli>A sampler supporting greedy, temperature, top-k, top-p, min-p, a repetition penalty and a seeded random generator\u003c/li>\u003cli>Benchmarks of tokens per second without and with the cache at prompt lengths of about 50, 500 and 2,000 tokens\u003c/li>\u003cli>A measured KV-cache size compared with the formula from config.json\u003c/li>\u003cli>Distribution experiments at several temperatures\u003c/li>\u003cli>A token-count and cost table across three tokenisers\u003c/li>\u003cli>A \u003ccode>REPORT.md\u003c/code> with tables, plots and explanations\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tokens and cost.\u003c/b> Count tokens for five texts (English prose, code, JSON, another language, a long number) with three tokenisers (one OpenAI encoding via \u003ccode>tiktoken\u003c/code>, two open models via \u003ccode>AutoTokenizer\u003c/code>). Turn the counts into a cost table at an example price per million tokens.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Naive loop.\u003c/b> Apply the chat template, then repeatedly run the full sequence through the model and append the argmax token until an end token or a limit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cached loop.\u003c/b> Prefill the prompt once with \u003ccode>use_cache=True\u003c/code>, then feed only the newest token with \u003ccode>past_key_values\u003c/code>. Check that greedy output is identical to the naive loop and to \u003ccode>model.generate(..., do_sample=False)\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sampler.\u003c/b> Implement the sampling controls from the sampling lesson on a 1-D logits tensor. Unit-test each one on a hand-made logits vector, for example that top-p 0.9 on probabilities 0.621, 0.229, 0.139 and 0.011 never returns the last token.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Speed.\u003c/b> Time both loops at three prompt lengths, generating 64 tokens each. Separately time prefill per token and decode per token with the cache.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cache size.\u003c/b> After a cached run, sum the bytes of all key and value tensors in the cache and compare with 2 × layers × KV heads × head dimension × bytes per value × cached tokens.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Distributions.\u003c/b> For one open-ended prompt, sample the first five tokens 200 times at temperatures 0.3, 0.7, 1.0 and 1.5; plot how many distinct continuations appear and the entropy of the first token. Generate ten full completions at three temperatures and describe the differences.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Report.\u003c/b> Explain every number: why the cache helps more for longer prompts, why prefill per token is so much cheaper than decode per token, and what each sampler setting did.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>python run.py\u003c/code> reproduces every table and plot from scratch\u003c/li>\u003cli>Cached and naive greedy outputs match exactly, and match \u003ccode>model.generate\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>The report shows the cache speed-up growing with prompt length, a measured cache size equal to the formula's prediction, and sampling results explained in your own words\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Batch three prompts of different lengths with left padding and an attention mask, and measure throughput against one at a time\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add constrained decoding: mask logits so the output must match a tiny JSON grammar such as \u003ccode>{\"answer\": \"yes\"}\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>{\"answer\": \"no\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement speculative decoding with a smaller draft model of the same family and measure the acceptance rate and speed-up\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a static cache with \u003ccode>torch.compile\u003c/code> and compare decode speed\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The core of the cached loop, tested with transformers 5 (older versions name the loading argument \u003ccode>torch_dtype\u003c/code> and expose cache tensors differently):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nMODEL = \"HuggingFaceTB/SmolLM2-135M-Instruct\"\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(MODEL)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(MODEL, dtype=torch.float32).eval()\n\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef generate_with_cache(ids, n_new, pick):\n    out = model(ids, use_cache=True)                    # prefill: the whole prompt once\n    past, new = out.past_key_values, []\n    for _ in range(n_new):\n        nxt = pick(out.logits[0, -1])\n        new.append(nxt)\n        if nxt == tok.eos_token_id:\n            break\n        out = model(torch.tensor([[nxt]]), past_key_values=past, use_cache=True)   # decode: one token\n        past = out.past_key_values\n    return new, past\n\nmessages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain what a KV cache is in two sentences.\"}]\nids = tok.apply_chat_template(messages, add_generation_prompt=True, return_tensors=\"pt\", return_dict=True)[\"input_ids\"]\nnew, past = generate_with_cache(ids, 64, pick=lambda logits: int(torch.argmax(logits)))\nprint(tok.decode(new, skip_special_tokens=True))\ncache_bytes = sum(l.keys.numel() + l.values.numel() for l in past.layers) * past.layers[0].keys.element_size()\nprint(past.get_seq_length(), \"tokens cached,\", cache_bytes, \"bytes\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>On a laptop CPU with this 135M model, the cached loop was about twice as fast as the naive one for a 41-token prompt and about six times as fast for a 492-token prompt, and the measured cache size matched the formula exactly (in 32-bit precision, so 4 bytes per value). Your numbers will differ; the trend should not.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>torch.inference_mode()\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>torch.no_grad()\u003c/code>, and warm up once before timing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Seed a \u003ccode>torch.Generator\u003c/code> and pass it to \u003ccode>torch.multinomial\u003c/code> so sampled runs are repeatable on the same machine.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
